WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.728
The eval-driven development loop.

2
00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:07.981
The working loop has five steps, and it’s worth memorizing because every lesson in this studio hangs off it.

3
00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:09.243
Look at data.

4
00:00:09.523 --> 00:00:13.149
Read real outputs (traces) before deciding what to measure.

5
00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:15.691
Lesson 2 is entirely about this.

6
00:00:15.971 --> 00:00:17.357
Define failure modes.

7
00:00:17.637 --> 00:00:23.611
Name the ways the system fails, specifically enough that two people would agree on whether a trace shows one.

8
00:00:23.891 --> 00:00:24.637
Measure.

9
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:30.016
Build a test set and graders for the failure modes that matter most, and record a baseline.

10
00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.427
Change one thing.

11
00:00:31.707 --> 00:00:34.757
A prompt edit, a retrieval setting, a model swap.

12
00:00:35.037 --> 00:00:35.677
One.

13
00:00:35.957 --> 00:00:42.869
Re-measure on the same set, compare with the baseline, keep or revert, and go back to step 1 with fresh traces.

14
00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:56.696
Husain’s essay frames this as a virtuous cycle of evaluating quality, debugging, and changing the system, and argues that doing all three well is what separates products that keep improving from products that plateau.

15
00:00:56.976 --> 00:01:01.584
“Change one thing” deserves a moment, because it is just experimental design.

16
00:01:01.864 --> 00:01:08.797
If you change the prompt and the model together and the score rises, you have one observation and two candidate causes.

17
00:01:09.077 --> 00:01:12.341
The eval can tell you the bundle helped; it can’t tell you why.

18
00:01:12.621 --> 00:01:18.019
On small test sets it may not even tell you that much, because a few cases flipping can be noise.

19
00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:25.851
Week 3 makes this quantitative with confidence intervals, following the approach in Evan Miller’s “Adding Error Bars to Evals.

20
00:01:26.131 --> 00:01:26.428
”.
